  "src": "Orc.md",
  "customTables": [
   {
    "heading": "d10 Orc Encounters (Tone Variance Table)",
    "die": "d10",
    "rows": [
     "| d10 | Encounter Premise | Campaign Tone / Mechanics |",
     "|:----|:------------------|:--------------------------|",
     "| **1** | **The Raid in Progress** | **Urgent:** The party arrives to find an orc warband mid-raid on a farmstead. The fighting is still happening. Choices made in the next three rounds determine who survives. |",
     "| **2** | **The Test of Strength** | **Social / Brutal:** The orc warlord doesn't want a battle — they want to know if the party is worth fighting *eventually*. They send their strongest warrior for a single combat. If the party wins with honor, they get passage. If they use magic or dirty tricks, all bets are off. |",
     "| **3** | **The Berserker Problem** | **Tactical:** A berserker has fully entered the One-Eyed's rage. It is attacking *everything* — including the other orcs. The rest of the warband is standing back. The party is between the berserker and the wall. |",
     "| **4** | **The Eye's Sermon** | **Tension Buildup:** The party hears chanting before they arrive. A Blind Prophet is mid-sermon, whipping the warband into divine fury. Every round the party waits to engage, the orcs gain an additional *bless* effect. |",
     "| **5** | **The Relentless Push** | **Mechanical Frustration:** The party hits an orc hard — killing blow — and it stays at 1 HP. Then a berserker does the same. Then a warrior does the same. The entire warband has fresh Relentless Endurance. This is a wave, not a skirmish. |",
     "| **6** | **The Mutual Enemy** | **Faction Complication:** The orc warband and the party want the same thing dead — the cult, the necromancer, the rival warlord. The orcs will work with the party for exactly as long as it takes, and not a minute longer. |",
     "| **7** | **The Hostage** | **Moral Dilemma:** The orc captain has a prisoner — a captive from a nearby settlement — and will kill them if attacked. They want tribute: the party's best weapon, given freely. |",
     "| **8** | **The Old Veteran** | **Character Depth:** A single orc warrior, separated from any warband, is camped in a corner of the dungeon. It is old, scarred, and moving slowly. It has Relentless Endurance and forty years of combat experience. It will fight if approached. It will also talk, if approached carefully. It is not entirely what orcs are expected to be. |",
     "| **9** | **The False Retreat** | **Tactical Trap:** The orcs break and run — but their berserker stays behind, and the Blind Prophet is casting *hold person* from a concealed position. The \"retreat\" was to draw the party into a chokepoint. |",
     "| **10** | **The One-Eyed's Chosen** | **Epic Scale:** The Prophet is mid-ritual, attempting to consecrate this location to the One-Eyed. If it completes the ritual (3 more rounds), every orc in the room is healed to full and gains Advantage on attacks for 1 hour. The Prophet is the priority. |"
    ]
   },
   {
    "heading": "d10 The Raided Ground (Investigation & Clues)",
    "die": "d10",
    "rows": [
     "| d10 | Discovery | Investigative Hook |",
     "|:----|:----------|:------------------|",
     "| **1** | **The Planted Spear** | A spear has been driven into the center of the room, wrapped in a strip of animal hide bearing the One-Eyed's mark. This territory has been formally claimed. A warband will return to check on their claim. |",
     "| **2** | **The Battle Map** | Scratched into the dirt floor is a crude tactical map of the dungeon — with the party's *current location* marked. The orcs had advance intelligence about the layout. |",
     "| **3** | **The Eye's Altar** | A rough stone altar covered in dried blood and one-eyed symbols. The Blind Prophet performed a sacrifice here to receive divine guidance. A DC 13 Religion check determines what the ritual was asking for — answers about a specific location, enemy, or timing. |",
     "| **4** | **The Tally Wall** | Hundreds of notches cut into a stone wall. A DC 12 History check estimates the warband has been staging out of this location for at least three months. |",
     "| **5** | **The Survivor Account** | A barely-alive victim can describe the warband's size, equipment, and direction of travel after the raid. They also mention one orc that stood apart — pale-skinned, quiet, and giving orders to the others. Not a typical orc. |",
     "| **6** | **The Abandoned Gear** | Quality weapons and armor left behind — not discarded in retreat, but *piled* carefully. The orcs left their heavy equipment here to move fast. They're traveling light and coming back. |",
     "| **7** | **The Branded Prisoner** | A captive bears an orc brand — a crude mark burned into their arm. The brand is a clan insignia, and a DC 14 History check identifies which clan. The brand also indicates the prisoner's \"status\" in orc terms — this one was marked as skilled labor, not a sacrifice. |",
     "| **8** | **The Tribute Collection** | A pile of specific items in the corner — not random loot, but a careful selection: iron tools, grain sacks, two horses' worth of saddlebags. This warband was collecting specific materials for a larger operation. |",
     "| **9** | **The Message Stone** | A flat stone with a message scratched in Orc runes. A DC 12 Languages or party member with Orc translates it: \"Site confirmed. Send the Prophet's battalion by the new moon.\" Someone is organizing multiple warbands. |",
     "| **10** | **The Half-Orc Witness** | A half-orc slave or prisoner in the camp who was taken in a previous raid. They speak Common fluently, despise their captors, and have been kept alive because they're useful as a translator. They know the warband's mission, chain of command, and, crucially, the name of whoever hired them. |"
    ]
   }
  ],
